from _lib import *

WEB1 = module("WEB1", "Web 1: how a web app works - you build one", "Web",
  "Every site you use is a program waiting at a door, answering questions with pages. Today you write one: it takes a form, keeps a list, remembers who you are, and runs on your Pi. Once you have built the thing, every weakness in other people's apps will make sense - because you will have made the same mistakes first, on purpose.",
  "Abid", "A tiny web app on her Pi - 'the family noticeboard' - where Abid can post a note from Canada and the family sees it in Dhaka, with her name in the footer.",
  [
    lesson("WEB1-L1", "A page is a document: HTML as structure, CSS as clothes",
      ["Write a page by hand with headings, a list, a link and an image, and explain what the browser does with the tags", "Add a few lines of CSS and explain the box model in one picture", "Open the browser's developer tools and read her own page's structure"],
      FROM_ZERO + LINUX + "No framework. nano ~/school/web/index.html: doctype, head with a title, body with h1, p, ul/li, a, img (a photo from her screenshots folder). Open in Firefox (file://). Tags are instructions to the browser about STRUCTURE; she inspects with F12 -> Inspector and sees the tree (like the file tree - everything is a tree). CSS: one style block: colours, a font, padding; the box model drawn (content, padding, border, margin). Then Flexbox Froggy (button) for 10 levels - layout as a game. Check: three tags and what each means; what F12 shows.",
      links=[link("Flexbox Froggy", "https://flexboxfroggy.com/", "CSS layout as a game; nothing to install")]),
    lesson("WEB1-L2", "A server answers: Flask, routes, and a page made by a program",
      ["Install Flask in a virtual environment and explain what a venv is for", "Write a route that returns a page with the current time; explain request -> function -> response", "Run it on the laptop, then on the Pi, and reach it from her phone"],
      FROM_ZERO + LINUX + "python3 -m venv ~/school/web/venv; source venv/bin/activate (a venv: this project's own shelf of packages, so projects do not fight); pip install flask. app.py: from flask import Flask; app = Flask(__name__); @app.route('/') def home(): return f'<h1>Faiza\\'s lab</h1><p>{datetime.now()}</p>'; app.run(host='0.0.0.0', port=5000). Mechanism: a request arrives at the door (NET1-L2), Flask matches the path to a function (a route), the function's return is the response body. curl -v it (she reads the headers she learned). On the Pi: copy with scp or git pull; run; ufw allow 5000 (she decides and says why; later nginx will sit in front). Check: route, request, response in her words."),
    code("WEB1-CODE1", "Code: the app's brain without the web - a noticeboard in a list",
      ["Write functions add_note(notes, who, text) and render(notes) that build the page's HTML as a string", "Keep the data separate from the page (a list of dicts; the HTML made from it)", "Escape user text and explain why (a note containing < must not become a tag)"],
      "import html\n\nnotes = []\n\ndef add_note(notes, who, text):\n    notes.append({\"who\": who, \"text\": text})\n\ndef render(notes):\n    out = \"<ul>\"\n    for n in notes:\n        out += f\"<li><b>{html.escape(n['who'])}</b>: {html.escape(n['text'])}</li>\"\n    return out + \"</ul>\"\n\nadd_note(notes, \"Abid\", \"Hello from Canada\")\nadd_note(notes, \"Faiza\", \"The Pi is <b>alive</b>\")\nprint(render(notes))\n",
      "Run it: the second note shows the literal <b>, not bold - that is escape doing its job. Then remove html.escape and run: the tags become real. That is the entire mechanism of the most common web bug (WEB2 names it); she has now seen it and fixed it before she knows its name. Boundary Question: what if text is 10,000 characters? (a length cap - she adds one). Save: this becomes the app's core."),
    lesson("WEB1-L3", "Forms and state: POST, a database file, and the noticeboard that remembers",
      ["Add a form (who, text) and a POST route; explain GET vs POST", "Store notes in SQLite with three SQL statements (CREATE, INSERT, SELECT) and explain a table", "Explain why the data must be checked on the server, never only in the browser"],
      FROM_ZERO + LINUX + "The form in the page (method=post); the route reads request.form; notes go into SQLite (define: a whole database in one file, built into Python): CREATE TABLE notes(id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY, who TEXT, text TEXT, at TEXT); INSERT with ? placeholders (she must use placeholders - WEB2 explains the disaster of string-building SQL; here she simply does it right); SELECT ... ORDER BY id DESC. GET asks for a page; POST sends data; a browser 'refresh' after POST re-sends - the redirect-after-POST pattern. Server-side checks: who and text not empty, length caps (the browser's checks can be skipped by anyone with curl - she proves it with curl -X POST). Check: GET vs POST; what a table is; why the server checks."),
    lesson("WEB1-L4", "Who are you: sessions, cookies, a login, and a password stored right",
      ["Add a simple login (one family password, stored as a hash, never plain) and explain the cookie that remembers the session", "Read the cookie in the browser's tools and explain what an attacker who stole it could do", "Set the cookie flags (HttpOnly, SameSite) and explain each"],
      FROM_ZERO + LINUX + "Mechanism: HTTP forgets everything between requests; a cookie is a note the server asks the browser to carry back each time; a session is the server's memory keyed by that note. Login: a form; the password compared to a stored HASH (werkzeug's generate_password_hash / check_password_hash - DEF1-L3's lesson in practice); on success, session['user'] = 'family'. F12 -> Storage -> the cookie: she sees it; a stolen cookie = being you until it expires - so HttpOnly (JavaScript cannot read it), SameSite (other sites cannot send it), Secure once HTTPS exists. The family password: generated, in Abid's vault and hers. Check: cookie, session, hash - and what HttpOnly prevents."),
    lesson("WEB1-L5", "Ship it: nginx in front, a service that restarts, and the app on the Pi for real",
      ["Run the app as a systemd service under its own user and explain why not root (DEF1-L2)", "Put nginx in front as a reverse proxy and explain what that buys (one door, logs, later TLS)", "Open the noticeboard from her phone and from Abid's call; read the log line it made"],
      FROM_ZERO + LINUX + LAB + "A systemd unit file: User=noticeboard (a service user), WorkingDirectory, ExecStart=venv/bin/python app.py, Restart=always; systemctl enable --now; status; journalctl -u noticeboard. nginx: a server block proxying / to 127.0.0.1:5000 - the app no longer listens on the network at all (ufw delete allow 5000) - one door, port 80. Reverse proxy (define: a doorman who passes requests inward). THE GIFT is live: Abid posts a note. Check: why a service user; what the proxy buys."),
    lesson("WEB1-H1", "Horizon: the web is apps talking to apps - APIs, the cloud, and who holds your data",
      ["Explain an API as a web app for programs (JSON in, JSON out) and read one (the school's own /api/ping)", "Explain 'the cloud' as other people's servers rented by the hour, and the trade-offs (speed, cost, control, who can read the disk)", "Ask who benefits, how would you break it, how would you fix it - for a service that stores family photos"],
      HORIZON + "She has used an API all along: the tutor's answers arrive as JSON from the school's server; curl /api/ping shows the shape. The cloud as mechanism: Vercel and Neon run the school on rented machines in a data centre - the school's own cost rules (the free tiers, the warm window) are a real-world example; her Pi is the opposite choice. Boundary Question: 'Your family's photos are on a company's servers. List what they can do with them, what the law says, and what you would do differently with your own Pi.'"),
    check("WEB1-C", "Web 1 checkpoint", ["html-and-css-basics", "routes-and-responses", "forms-get-post", "sqlite-and-placeholders", "sessions-and-cookies"], count=10),
    teach("WEB1-T", "Explain to a 10-year-old what happens when they press 'post' on your noticeboard: the form, the server, the database, the page. Then explain to Abid why the password is stored as a hash and what the cookie is for. Say it out loud first, then write it. 5-7 sentences."),
  ],
  read={"url": "https://flask.palletsprojects.com/en/stable/quickstart/", "title": "Read: the Flask quickstart - the framework you will build with"})


MODULES = [WEB1]
